[38;2;124;124;124mThe problem: Let $x_1, \ldots, x_n$ be real with $\sum x_i^2 = 1$. For every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minteger $k \geq 2$, there exist integers $a_1, \ldots, a_n$, not all zero, with [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$|a_i| \leq k-1$ for all $i$, and $|\sum a_i x_i| \leq \frac{(k-1)\sqrt{n}}{k^n [0m[38;2;124;124;124m- 1}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic pigeonhole/Minkowski-type argument. Let me think about the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mapproach.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider all vectors $a = (a_1, \ldots, a_n)$ with $0 \leq a_i \leq k-1$. There [0m[38;2;124;124;124mare $k^n$ such vectors. The sums $S(a) = a_1 x_1 + \cdots + a_n x_n$ range over [0m[38;2;124;124;124msome interval. By pigeonhole, two of them are close, and their difference gives [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe result.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe sums $S(a)$ for $a_i \in \{0, 1, \ldots, k-1\}$: the maximum value is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$(k-1)\sum_{x_i > 0} x_i \leq (k-1)\sqrt{n}$ (by Cauchy-Schwarz, since $\sum [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx_i^2 = 1$ implies $|\sum x_i| \leq \sqrt{n}$, and $\sum_{x_i>0} x_i \leq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\sqrt{n}$). Similarly the minimum is $\geq -(k-1)\sqrt{n}$. So all sums lie in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124man interval of length at most $2(k-1)\sqrt{n}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe have $k^n$ sums in an interval of length $2(k-1)\sqrt{n}$. By pigeonhole, two[0m[38;2;124;124;124msums differ by at most $\frac{2(k-1)\sqrt{n}}{k^n - 1}$.[0m

