[38;2;124;124;124mLet me reconsider the range. Actually, the sums range from $\sum_{i: x_i < 0} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(k-1) x_i$ to $\sum_{i: x_i > 0} (k-1) x_i$. The total range is $(k-1)(\sum_{x_i[0m[38;2;124;124;124m> 0} x_i - \sum_{x_i < 0} x_i) = (k-1) \sum |x_i| \leq (k-1)\sqrt{n}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait! The range is $(k-1) \sum_{i} |x_i|$? No. Let me be more careful.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe minimum sum is achieved when $a_i = k-1$ for $x_i < 0$ and $a_i = 0$ for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$x_i > 0$ (and $a_i$ anything for $x_i = 0$). So minimum $= (k-1)\sum_{x_i < 0} [0m[38;2;124;124;124mx_i$. Maximum $= (k-1)\sum_{x_i > 0} x_i$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mRange $= (k-1)(\sum_{x_i > 0} x_i - \sum_{x_i < 0} x_i) = (k-1) \sum_i |x_i| [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\leq (k-1) \sqrt{n}$ by Cauchy-Schwarz.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the range is at most $(k-1)\sqrt{n}$, not $2(k-1)\sqrt{n}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWith $k^n$ points in an interval of length $(k-1)\sqrt{n}$, two points differ by[0m[38;2;124;124;124mat most $\frac{(k-1)\sqrt{n}}{k^n - 1}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThat gives exactly the bound we need![0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write this up carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider the $k^n$ vectors $a = (a_1, \ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_i \in \{0, 1, [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\ldots, k-1\}$. For each such vector, define $S(a) = a_1 x_1 + \cdots + a_n [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx_n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe minimum value of $S$ is achieved by setting $a_i = k-1$ when $x_i < 0$ and [0m
